WEBVTT
NOTE OpenIE Research TV v3.0 — rce (45s) Narrator Alice

1
00:00:00.280 --> 00:00:04.026
First, you write a program once as one clear blueprint,

2
00:00:04.026 --> 00:00:07.976
and the computer generates matching code across languages.

3
00:00:08.416 --> 00:00:13.172
Second, every possible input must have a declared outcome up front,

4
00:00:13.172 --> 00:00:16.508
preventing hidden crashes before anything runs.

5
00:00:16.948 --> 00:00:21.676
Third, every repair that passes the checker is saved and reused,

6
00:00:21.676 --> 00:00:24.261
so you never pay twice for one fix.

7
00:00:24.701 --> 00:00:30.654
In ordinary software, even simple division of negative numbers gives different answers

8
00:00:30.654 --> 00:00:35.015
in Python and C. Instead of rewriting logic four times by hand,

9
00:00:35.015 --> 00:00:40.345
the Reality Code Engine checks one master blueprint and prints matching code.

10
00:00:40.785 --> 00:00:48.283
Checking one blueprint takes just seven milliseconds and forty-nine millijoules on an Apple M5 Max chip counter,

11
00:00:48.283 --> 00:00:51.965
turning software reliability into an upfront guarantee.
